<!--
    Device VINTF manifest for the recovery ramdisk.

    Without this file NO HIDL service in recovery can register. libhidlbase is
    built with -DENFORCE_VINTF_MANIFEST, so registerAsService() first asks
    hwservicemanager for the interface's transport and bails out when it is not
    HWBINDER:
        "Service android.hardware.keymaster@4.0::IKeymasterDevice/default must
         be in VINTF manifest in order to register/get."  -> UNKNOWN_ERROR
    and android.hardware.keymaster@4.0-service-qti exits, so FBE decryption
    never gets a keymaster.

    The transport comes from the device HAL manifest, and
    VintfObject::fetchDeviceHalManifest() only reads the fragment directory
    /vendor/etc/vintf/manifest/ when /vendor/etc/vintf/manifest.xml itself
    parsed OK (system/libvintf/VintfObject.cpp). With no main manifest there is
    no /odm and no legacy /vendor/manifest.xml either, so the whole device
    manifest came out empty - which is why the boot@1.1/boot@1.2/health@2.1
    fragments the build already installs there were ignored too. They are
    picked up as soon as this file exists.

    keymaster is @4.0 only: that is what the device actually registers on stock
    (lshal: android.hardware.keymaster@4.0::IKeymasterDevice/default), and
    getService() matches the fqname exactly, so declaring @4.1 instead would
    hang every client.
-->
<manifest version="2.0" type="device">
    <hal format="hidl">
        <name>android.hardware.keymaster</name>
        <transport>hwbinder</transport>
        <version>4.0</version>
        <interface>
            <name>IKeymasterDevice</name>
            <instance>default</instance>
        </interface>
        <fqname>@4.0::IKeymasterDevice/default</fqname>
    </hal>
</manifest>
